import eliot.file.Path
import eliot.file.File
import eliot.collection.List

/**
 * What a finished process left behind: its exit code and, when the output was captured, everything
 * it wrote to the two output streams.
 *
 * Representation is platform-owned, like every other base-layer type.
 */
type ProcessResult

/**
 * The code the process exited with. Zero conventionally means success, but the meaning is the
 * program's, so this is data rather than a failure — see `Process` for why a non-zero exit does not
 * raise.
 */
def exitCode(result: ProcessResult): Int

/**
 * Everything the process wrote to standard output, or `""` when its output was inherited rather
 * than captured.
 */
def standardOutput(result: ProcessResult): String

/**
 * Everything the process wrote to standard error, or `""` when its output was inherited.
 */
def standardError(result: ProcessResult): String

/**
 * The process model a hosted operating system offers: running other programs, and what this one
 * reports when it ends.
 *
 * The effect a language-owned build tool is built on: resolution shells out to `git`, the build
 * spawns the compiler as its own process, and the tool reports to the shell that invoked it whether
 * any of that worked. The two spawning operations take the command as a **list of arguments**,
 * never as a string to be re-split — quoting rules are the one part of process spawning that is
 * different on every platform and wrong in every hand-rolled implementation.
 *
 * A target with no process model simply ships no default `implement Process`, so a `uses Process`
 * program fails at the run boundary there — the layer system is the capability, exactly as it is
 * for `Environment` and `FileSystem`. That is also why an exit code may be named here at all: a
 * platform that has no notion of one is a platform on which none of this effect exists.
 *
 * ```eliot
 * def tags(repository: Path) uses Process, Throw[IoError]: String = {
 *    val listed = run(command("git").withArgument("ls-remote").withArgument("--tags"), repository)
 *    listed.standardOutput
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * **A non-zero exit is not a failure.** It is the ordinary way a program reports a decision — `git
 * ls-remote` on a missing ref, a compiler on a program with errors — and a caller almost always
 * wants to read the output before deciding what it means. Only *not being able to run the program
 * at all* raises: the executable is missing, the directory does not exist, the process is killed by
 * the operating system. That is an `IoError` on `Throw[IoError]`, the same channel `FileSystem`
 * uses, so a program that touches both the filesystem and other processes has one error type and
 * one `catch`, not two.
 *
 * There is no process *handle* and no streaming, by the same reasoning as `eliot.file`'s whole-file
 * surface: both spawning operations start a process and wait for it, so nothing can be left
 * running. Interactive or long-lived children are future work, and will need the bracket machinery
 * that file handles are also waiting on.
 */
effect Process {
   /**
    * Run `command` in `workingDirectory`, wait for it to finish, and capture what it wrote.
    *
    * The first element of `command` is the executable, looked up on the platform's search path when
    * it is not a path; the rest are its arguments, passed through untouched. An empty `command`
    * raises.
    */
   def run(command: List[String], workingDirectory: Path) uses Throw[IoError]: ProcessResult

   /**
    * Run `command` in `workingDirectory` with the standard streams **inherited** — the child writes
    * straight to this program's own output — and yield its exit code.
    *
    * For a child whose output belongs to the user rather than to the program: the compiler's
    * diagnostics, a test run's report. Nothing is captured, so `standardOutput` of a `run` result
    * would have nowhere to come from; that is why this yields the code alone.
    */
   def runInheritingIo(command: List[String], workingDirectory: Path) uses Throw[IoError]: Int

   /**
    * Register the code this program reports to whoever started it, zero conventionally meaning
    * success and anything else failure.
    *
    * Nothing happens at the call: the code is **recorded**, and the program carries straight on. So
    * a program decides its outcome where the decision is actually made, goes on to write its
    * summary, close what it opened and return normally, and nothing it was in the middle of is
    * skipped. Registering again replaces what was registered before — the last decision is the one
    * reported — and a program that never registers reports zero.
    *
    * The registered code is read when the program **terminates normally**. A program that dies
    * instead — an uncaught failure reaching the entry point — reports whatever the platform makes of
    * that, and what was registered is never read.
    *
    * A platform may narrow the code: a POSIX system reports the low eight bits of it, so a program
    * that wants to be understood everywhere registers something in `0 .. 255`. Nothing checks this
    * yet.
    *
    * ```eliot
    * // Compile one module, answering whether it succeeded.
    * def compile(module: Module) uses Console, Process: Bool
    *
    * // Build every module, and tell the shell that invoked us if any of them failed. The
    * // registration sits where the decision is made; the summary below it still runs.
    * def build(modules: List[Module]) uses Console, Process: Unit = {
    *    val succeeded = modules.all(compile)
    *
    *    if(succeeded, unit) else registerExitCode(1)
    *
    *    printLine("built " ++ show(modules.size) ++ " modules")
    * }
    * ```
    */
   def registerExitCode(code: Int): Unit
}

/**
 * A one-word command — the common case, spelled without building a list by hand.
 */
def command(executable: String): List[String] = append(empty, executable)

/**
 * `command` with one more argument appended, so a command line reads left to right:
 * `command("git").withArgument("ls-remote").withArgument("--tags")`.
 */
def withArgument(argument: String, command: List[String]): List[String] = append(command, argument)
